WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:09.840
Hi, welcome back to the Wheel of Life online retreat,

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:16.799
And we're reaching session 5.

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:23.130
The name of this session is "The Best Time to Trigger Karmas."

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.629
And the picture you see here is

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:35.909
somebody talking to a person who's on their deathbed.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:49.169
And this session we're going to follow the Tibetan text pretty closely.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:59.189
If I say something that's kind of intriguing or interesting, to me especially,

00:01:03.340 --> 00:01:07.529
then I'm going to stop and we're going to go deeper.

00:01:09.490 --> 00:01:11.490
I know it's slower.

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:16.439
I know some of you don't want to learn the ancient language

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:27.000
and I don't care. So I'm going to do it my way. Okay, so here we go!

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:31.039
su dep-pay du ni
(GSOS 'DEBS PA'I DUS NI)

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:35.670
I'm going to make my cursor bigger, hang on one second.

00:02:01.939 --> 00:02:04.419
su dep-pay du ni

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:13.449
means: at what point in the death process do our karmas get activated?

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:21.639
chi khar ('CHI KHAR) means "at the moment of death."

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:30.369
rang gi drenpa-am shen gyi dren du chukpa gang rung gi go ne 
(RANG GIS GRAN PA'AM GZHAN GYIS DRAN DU BCUG PA GANG RUNG GI SGO NAS)
means: it doesn't matter

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.759
whether you remember to think about something,

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.750
or your friend or your relative is standing there next to you as you die,

00:02:48.709 --> 00:02:54.309
and they... dren du chukpa (DRAN DU BCUG PA) means: they remind you, they say "don't don't forget to have a good seed."

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:00.880
And it could be

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:08.078
depa dang nying je (DAD PA DANG SNYING RJE) means: maybe they remind you "try to have faith towards your teacher."

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.890
Or try to have compassion for people.

00:03:17.120 --> 00:03:22.509
So those are an example of gewa (DGE BA). gewa means a good seed at the moment of death.

00:03:28.850 --> 00:03:30.999
But it could be du chak ('DOD CHAGS) or kong tro (KHONG KHRO)--

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.630
it could be strong desire or strong anger.

00:03:41.269 --> 00:03:44.439
Which is mi geway chu (MI DGE BA'I CHOS); it means "to die with bad seeds."

00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:55.839
gang run (GANG RUNG) means: it doesn't matter whether we're talking about good seeds when you die, or bad seeds when you die.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:09.129
But we're talking about du she rakpa jungway bardu ('DU SHES RAGS PA 'BYUNG BA'I BAR DU), which means still your mind is in this world.

00:04:14.990 --> 00:04:17.619
You're still generally seeing the same world.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:23.230
It's still your relatives standing around you.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:30.010
It's still the hospital room where they put you when you got sick.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:39.519
sem la chupa yin (SEMS LA SPYOD PA YIN) means your mind is operating on this level of normal reality.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:46.720
Now let's go back to the text.

00:04:48.860 --> 00:04:54.610
du she rakpa gak ne ('DU SHES RAGS PA 'GAGS NAS)--"when that rough awareness stops."

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:02.649
And you move into chi sem tramoy ('CHI SEMS PHRA MO'I) ... means "a very subtle

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:09.730
awareness of death." It's a primitive awareness that you've had for many, many, many billions of lifetimes.

00:05:20.330 --> 00:05:24.039
That primitive--almost animal--state of mind

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:33.399
is lung ma ten (LUNG MA BSTAN). lung ma ten means "karmically neutral."

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:44.019
It's as if you were drugged ... deeply drugged or something.

00:05:50.480 --> 00:05:53.709
ge mi ge gang yang dren mi nu (DGE MI DGE GANG YANG DRAN MI NUS)--you can't even

00:05:55.130 --> 00:06:00.159
frame the thought ... "you can't even think about good karma or bad karma."

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.239
Your mental state is just like very drunk or something.

00:06:13.540 --> 00:06:15.540
de na (DES NA)--"therefore,"

00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:19.230
chi ka may sem la depa sok jung na 
('CHI KHA MA'I SEMS LA DAD PA SOGS BYUNG NA) ...

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:27.600
If at that moment--just as your mind is about to switch to the more primitive state of mind--

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:37.500
if you can activate a good seed,

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:43.710
that's the moment that you should try.

00:06:46.870 --> 00:06:49.919
So, you and your loved ones,

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.080
you should try at that moment

00:06:57.790 --> 00:07:01.019
just as you're losing awareness of this world.

00:07:05.830 --> 00:07:08.100
ne che (GNAD CHE) means it's "extremely important"

00:07:10.630 --> 00:07:17.820
to try to go out, we say 'go out of the picture,' with a pure state--a virtuous state of mind.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:29.669
Then Pabongka Rinpoche, who is the author here, the speaker--

00:07:33.550 --> 00:07:35.700
who was one of my teacher's teachers

00:07:38.289 --> 00:07:40.289
when my teacher was a young man--

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:47.609
he addresses a frequently asked question:

00:07:51.449 --> 00:07:58.609
gyun du gewa je khen gyi chupa shik yin kyang
(RGYUN DU DGE BA BYED MKHAN GYI CHOS PA ZHIG YIN KYANG) ... now, you may be a Dharma person--

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:04.980
and the Tibetan is literally "Dharma person," chupa (CHOS PA).

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:16.640
chu (CHOS) means "Dharma" and chupa is an unusual word, but it means "person who likes to do Dharma."

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:28.460
So gyun du gewa je khen (RGYUN DU DGE BA BYED MKHAN) you can be a "person who their whole life was a pretty good person."

00:08:32.430 --> 00:08:37.880
And you were a chupa zhik (CHOS PA ZHIG)--a "Dharma person."

00:08:40.589 --> 00:08:43.360
For $20, what's the word kyang (KYANG)?

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:51.440
[Stanley: "but"] Dang, he's correct again!

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:58.099
If you get rich, you have to spread it to all the online students.  Okay, even though

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.560
you may be a ... maybe you were a pretty good person your whole life,

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:07.220
kyang--

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:15.349
But, chi kar ('CHI KHAR), "at the moment of that death" when you lose this world consciousness,

00:09:23.700 --> 00:09:27.890
kong tro tabu kye (KHONG KHRO LTA BU SKYES), if you "suddenly get an emotion of anger" at somebody,

00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:35.870
chi sem mi gewa la su tab ('CHI SEMS MI DGE BA LA GSOS BTAB), that karma will be triggered.

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.549
And remember Vasubandhu said...

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:47.419
yeah, closer to death. And I think he's talking about this moment.

00:09:49.079 --> 00:09:52.489
Sometimes I taught it to people, like in the last week or the last two weeks.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:02.540
But this one seems to be in that crucial few minutes when you are losing

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.799
awareness of this world, and you're going into that primitive state of mind.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:22.760
So, here's the bad news.

00:10:23.279 --> 00:10:29.148
ngun du mi gewa min (SNGON DU MI DGE BA SMIN), the "bad karma will ripen first." 
That new bad karma.

00:10:32.279 --> 00:10:33.380
ngen song du dro (NGAN SONG DU 'GRO), you will go to one of the three lower births:

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:43.550
Animal, hungry spirit, or hell realms.

00:10:46.920 --> 00:10:48.920
And here's the good news.

00:10:50.790 --> 00:10:52.790
gyun du dikpa je khen la-ang (RGYUN DU SDIG PA BYED MKHAN LA'ANG),

00:10:54.290 --> 00:10:58.219
but if you're the kind of "person who spent their whole life doing bad things,"

00:11:02.279 --> 00:11:07.969
de rik dre (DES RIGS 'GRE). de rik dre means "the same pattern applies" to you, luckily.

00:11:12.959 --> 00:11:16.609
So, if you've been a not very good person your whole life,

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.190
but in the last minutes of your life,

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:26.539
you know, you take refuge in the Three Jewels,

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:36.440
you think of some holy being. And there's a great Christian custom

00:11:39.779 --> 00:11:42.708
that the priest will try to be there as you die,

00:11:45.900 --> 00:11:52.879
and they will whisper in your ear, "Imagine all your bad deeds are clean."

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:02.210
In Buddhism, we would also say

00:12:04.860 --> 00:12:08.360
if your last thoughts are pure and you regret your bad deeds,

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:19.310
then that last karma would take priority over a whole life of not very good karma.

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:31.940
So let's try to die well, okay?

00:12:34.860 --> 00:12:38.900
And I think that would involve 

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.849
training your family to help you at that moment.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:59.510
dey kab su de gro dang ngen dro gang du drowa sok ni (DE'I SKABS SU BDE 'GRO DANG NGAN 'GRO GANG DU 'GRO BA SOGS NI), now "at that moment"--as your consciousness shifts--

00:13:05.070 --> 00:13:07.339
if we're standing there watching you,

00:13:09.120 --> 00:13:14.270
is there any "indication that you're going to a good place or a bad place"?

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:21.800
This is kind of subtle.

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:30.049
de dror dro na dru me ne yar nying gar duwa dang (BDE 'GROR 'GRO NA DROD SMAD NAS YAR SNYING GAR BSDU BA DANG)--"If you're going to go to a good place,

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:35.420
the warmth of your body

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:41.689
withdraws from the feet to the heart."

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.480
So, that person's feet get cold first.

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:53.000
Then their lower leg.

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.370
Then their thigh.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:58.380
Then their abdomen.

00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:00.869
And then the warmth

00:14:01.540 --> 00:14:03.540
dissipates at the heart.

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:07.530
Now, if you're going to go to a bad place ...

00:14:12.370 --> 00:14:18.690
ngen dror dro na de le dokpa sok (NGAN 'GROR 'GRO NA DE LAS LDOG PA SOGS), and the opposite would be the case if you're going to a bad place.

00:14:24.130 --> 00:14:30.660
So, some people would say that the warmth retreats. The cold comes in this way.

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:42.690
So, there's different options of the warmth coming in, and the warmth coming out.

00:14:52.090 --> 00:14:58.619
ne chu che chung sok la takpe she (GNAD GCOD CHE CHUNG SOGS LA BRTAG PAS SHES), you can also get an indication

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:08.880
of where the person's going to go by how much ne chu kyi dukngel 
(GNAD GCOD KYI SDUG BSNGAL) they have.

00:15:11.680 --> 00:15:14.100
And this is a very ...

00:15:15.640 --> 00:15:17.999
this is an idiom in the ancient languages.

00:15:20.170 --> 00:15:22.170
dukngel (SDUG BSNGAL) is easy, that means

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:25.860
"suffering."

00:15:26.260 --> 00:15:28.260
ne chu (GNAD GCOD) means:

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.340
"the crucial points in your body are getting cut."

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:40.799
And there's Sanskrit; it's called marmaccheda.

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:45.809
So, marma, you know, it's a "crucial point."

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.630
For example, there's an artery right here.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:56.850
With a small cut,

00:15:58.959 --> 00:16:00.959
you can kill a person.

00:16:01.579 --> 00:16:04.149
And nobody can fix you.

00:16:06.470 --> 00:16:08.709
Then that's called marma--

00:16:10.639 --> 00:16:12.639
crucial point.

00:16:14.329 --> 00:16:19.659
Literally means "death point," and mar, the Sanskrit mar, means like "murder" or "mortuary."

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:30.940
And cheda means "cut," like in vajra chedika. And for zero dollars...

00:16:33.649 --> 00:16:41.469
Yeah, "to cut." And so when those crucial chakra points get cut,

00:16:45.709 --> 00:16:49.179
when the life dies in those crucial places,

00:16:54.139 --> 00:16:56.139
there's special ...

00:16:57.110 --> 00:16:59.110
sharp pains.

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:04.479
And they say it's worse than any pain you can have in your whole life.

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:13.329
So, if the person is dying and they're like screaming really loud ...

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:24.189
it means ne chu kyi dukngel, those crucial points are being cut.

00:17:26.569 --> 00:17:28.569
And

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.480
They are going down.

00:17:32.600 --> 00:17:36.790
Okay, then he explains the word ne chu, which we did already.

00:17:41.630 --> 00:17:48.429
ga shik chi ka ne trul nang shar te ('GA' ZHIG 'CHI KA NAS 'KHRUL SNANG SHAR TE)--"some people when they die start to have hallucinations."

00:17:55.130 --> 00:17:57.640
sharwa (SHAR BA) here means they have the impression--

00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:04.030
and this is a strange expression that

00:18:04.940 --> 00:18:08.440
blocks of Chinese tea are being stacked on their chest.

00:18:16.250 --> 00:18:18.609
Then they feel very heavy, crushing their chest.

00:18:22.430 --> 00:18:26.019
Or some big guy is shoving down on their chest.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:34.329
Then Pabongka Rinpoche gives a few real examples that he ... maybe he saw these, I don't know.

00:18:40.850 --> 00:18:43.449
ngar hla sar shung say chak dzu nang sen 
(SNGAR LHA SAR GZHUNG SA'I PHYAG MDZOD NANG ZAN)--

00:18:46.100 --> 00:18:52.839
Before, there was a government official in the city of Lhasa.

00:18:55.850 --> 00:18:58.990
zhung (GZHUNG) here means "federal government."

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:09.339
Then they use a very unusual Tibetan word called nang sen (NANG ZAN).

00:19:15.980 --> 00:19:18.189
It means "eat inside."

00:19:20.120 --> 00:19:22.120
It means a low government official who didn't get a salary.

00:19:33.590 --> 00:19:35.590
They just get food.

00:19:36.980 --> 00:19:40.839
Okay, that's all. Or a person who's a

00:19:42.530 --> 00:19:46.209
servant or a slave in those days--they don't get a salary, they just feed them.

00:19:53.870 --> 00:19:57.219
Then ja kuwa (JA BRKU BA), he "stole some tea."

00:20:00.230 --> 00:20:04.060
shik chi khar ja gam mangpu nen ongway trul nang ngenpa jung te
(ZHIG 'CHI KHAR JA SGAM MANG POS MNAN 'ONG BA'I 'KHRUL SNANG NGAN PA BYUNG STE)

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:13.390
Then as he died, he had this hallucination that there were boxes of Chinese tea stacked on his chest.

00:20:22.570 --> 00:20:25.379
Then he had these terrible hallucinations.

00:20:28.210 --> 00:20:35.789
shiwa sik tsul (SHI BA GZIGS TSUL) means our Lama described in his teaching how he had witnessed that himself.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:48.659
tsang ne punpo shik (GTZANG NAS DPON PO ZHIG), and then there was

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.559
an official from Tsang, which is the state north of Lhasa.

00:21:05.260 --> 00:21:07.290
chi khar ('CHI KHAR) means "as he was dying,"

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:17.430
ngay teng ne mi mangpu nen jung ser te 
(NGA'I STENG NAS MI MANG POS MNAN BYUNG ZER TE), 
he yelled out, "there are many strong men crushing" his chest

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:29.009
jik trak gi ngang ne tse phupa ('JIGS SKRAG GI NGANG NAS TSE 'PHOS PA) 
and he died--which really means his consciousness shifted levels,

00:21:33.490 --> 00:21:36.059
with great jik trak ('JIGS SKRAG)--means "terror."

00:21:38.260 --> 00:21:40.260
So jik ('JIGS) means "fear."

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:44.670
We have a good dear friend called Jigme ('JIGS MED),

00:21:47.279 --> 00:21:52.069
and that means "fearless." And the Sanskrit is? ...

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:58.850
abhaya, and that was the name of Naropa.

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.390
His [Naropa's] original name.

00:22:03.870 --> 00:22:09.319
Okay, gewa jepa-ang (DGE BA BYED PA'ANG)--now if you're a person who "did some good karmas,"

00:22:12.960 --> 00:22:16.549
even if you're a trangpo (SPRANG PO), which means "a beggar."

00:22:18.659 --> 00:22:23.839
And here's another idiom that has sen (ZAN), meaning "to eat" in it: lam sen (LAM ZAN).

00:22:28.289 --> 00:22:31.219
So guess what's a lam sen--without looking at my notes.

00:22:35.250 --> 00:22:39.799
lam? [path] Yeah, he eats on the road--it means he's poor.

00:22:44.700 --> 00:22:48.830
Like, he's sitting on the side of the road, he asks if you have an extra piece of pizza.

00:22:52.799 --> 00:22:56.388
And then he just sits there in the dirt and eats the piece of pizza.

00:23:01.260 --> 00:23:05.329
So forget it, he's not a big government official.

00:23:09.240 --> 00:23:12.079
He's just sitting there, guk (BSGUGS) means "waiting," and

00:23:13.169 --> 00:23:17.898
du (SDOD) means sitting on the side of the road "hoping" someone will give him some piece of pizza or something.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:30.590
chiwar nyewa na ('CHI BAR NYE BA NA)--and then he was "close to dying," he "started to die."

00:23:34.350 --> 00:23:38.000
khang sang dang u-karpoy nangwa jungwa dang
(KHANG BZANG DANG 'OD DKAR PO'I SNANG BA BYUNG BA DANG)--He saw

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:44.809
palaces and beautiful white light, and he saw some kind of paradises.

00:23:52.200 --> 00:23:55.010
So, if you have good karmic seeds,

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:03.410
just before the mind shifts levels,

00:24:07.170 --> 00:24:10.789
you will have some extraordinarily beautiful visions.

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:17.580
And chu sang du (CHU BZANG DU)--chu sang is like a district in Tibet.

00:24:23.970 --> 00:24:27.740
mani mangpo tunpay gen-mo (MA nI MANG PO BTON PA'I RGAN MO)--there was an old, old lady

00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:34.009
but she's doing a lot of om mani padme hungs--with a good heart

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:44.869
chi kar ('CHI KHAR) when she died, she had many beautiful visions.

00:24:48.060 --> 00:24:51.560
That tsul (TSUL) here means our Lama described those to us.

00:24:56.010 --> 00:24:58.219
And then he ends this segment.

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:05.239
de tabuy taknam ma chiy gong ne ngundro tar jungwa yin
(DE LTA BU'I RTAGS RNAMS MA 'CHI'I GONG NAS SNGON 'GRO LTAR 'BYUNG BA YIN)

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:08.719
When you get these kinds of visions,

00:25:14.340 --> 00:25:18.199
it's not actually the moment of death.

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:24.469
They are kind of like preliminaries.

00:25:28.230 --> 00:25:32.660
Yeah, so it's not like you have that vision and you go there immediately or something like that.

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:41.780
It's while your mind is still in this world,

00:25:45.710 --> 00:25:52.569
you will have those visions, but then the mind shifts to that very like almost animal state of mind.

00:25:57.470 --> 00:26:04.270
So, to summarize the point of this section, and don't forget the picture of a person talking to someone dying ...

00:26:13.730 --> 00:26:14.230
You know, a lot of people ask lamas, including my lama,

00:26:14.230 --> 00:26:17.090
after someone dies, "What can I do to help them?"

00:26:29.090 --> 00:26:31.930
But what they're saying here is, a very good time to help someone

00:26:37.670 --> 00:26:41.439
is before their awareness of this world collapses--

00:26:45.680 --> 00:26:48.789
talk to them about the good things they've done in their life.

00:26:53.420 --> 00:26:55.539
Maybe they did many bad things in their life.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:27:01.269
This is not the time to talk about them.

00:27:04.010 --> 00:27:06.489
This is the time to say something encouraging.

00:27:11.120 --> 00:27:16.030
And any little thing that they did that's a good thing,

00:27:20.510 --> 00:27:22.060
praise them and say, "Oh, you should be so happy!"

00:27:27.110 --> 00:27:29.110
"You remember you did that good thing?"

00:27:30.520 --> 00:27:36.239
"That was amazing!" So what this section is talking about,

00:27:38.050 --> 00:27:40.259
if you want to help other people,

00:27:41.740 --> 00:27:43.570
then

00:27:43.570 --> 00:27:47.519
remind them--while they're still aware of this world--

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:52.320
about the good things they did.

00:27:54.220 --> 00:27:59.669
Then there are descriptions in some traditions about powa (PHO BA).

00:28:02.800 --> 00:28:08.700
And these are special methods to use after you lose this world's awareness.

00:28:15.670 --> 00:28:19.200
When the awareness drops down to almost animal level,

00:28:25.150 --> 00:28:29.129
you have a standard set of hallucinations.

00:28:34.420 --> 00:28:41.789
And they are connected to the dissolution of the elements in your body.

00:28:48.460 --> 00:28:55.499
sa chu me lung (SA CHU ME RLUNG)--as the earth element collapses in your physical body,

00:29:00.520 --> 00:29:06.210
your primitive mind has a perception of an earthquake or landslides.

00:29:14.650 --> 00:29:16.710
And then, as the water element dissolves,

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:25.469
you have this perception of floods--you carried away under floods, you're drowning.

00:29:30.640 --> 00:29:32.640
Then, as the fire element dissolves,

00:29:34.840 --> 00:29:39.720
you have this hallucination of a desert and fire.

00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:48.120
And then lung (RLUNG), as the wind element starts to conclude,

00:29:52.720 --> 00:29:56.520
first there's a tornado or tempest,

00:29:58.600 --> 00:30:03.480
but then you see a very ... like a still candle flame.

00:30:07.630 --> 00:30:09.809
And it's steady--it's not moving at all.

00:30:12.370 --> 00:30:19.260
So those are standard hallucinations that everybody has as they die.

00:30:25.450 --> 00:30:31.170
And we described them in a previous lam rim section.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:42.990
And if you're interested in them, Tim is going to put up a web address.

00:30:47.559 --> 00:30:51.509
If you have some extra time during your online retreat,

00:30:55.690 --> 00:30:57.720
then you can go listen to those descriptions.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:05.130
For a person who has practiced many, many, many years

00:31:08.740 --> 00:31:10.740
how to die,

00:31:12.309 --> 00:31:15.269
there is a few moments' space

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:25.890
in that insanity where you could see emptiness.

00:31:30.100 --> 00:31:33.150
But I would say the chances of that are like one in a million.

00:31:38.020 --> 00:31:40.109
So the recommendation of my teachers is

00:31:43.030 --> 00:31:50.790
don't wait until you're in the middle of these intense hallucinations where the whole world is collapsing.

00:31:59.860 --> 00:32:01.890
Don't wait until then to try to see emptiness.

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.530
Because the odds are like a million to one against you.

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:17.310
It's better sitting at home watching a pleasant online retreat,

00:32:21.880 --> 00:32:23.880
with a comfortable couch,

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:28.380
and nice incense,

00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:32.970
nice quiet meditation room--

00:32:36.100 --> 00:32:38.100
than try to see emptiness.

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:41.460
Okay, we'll see you in the next segment.

00:32:42.580 --> 00:32:47.729
And thank you for being with us, and thank you, Stanley, for translating.

